// The thread kind's step (thread.kind): owed by the watcher, which coordd answers itself, as it does the clerk's. It
// runs `sort` below once, statelessly, with the model policy names, and answers `intake` with its verdict; a call
// that fails, or answers out of shape, is answered Thread. There is no one-off (design-two-systems §16, 3): words
// that go on no open ticket are a thread of their own. OPEN is coordd's: the open tickets TO takes part in, one a
// line, with its kind and title; for words typed to nobody (TO is then whoever fills lead), the open tickets the
// owner takes part in. Unsure(tickets) (paper-research m1249) is for words that could go on more than one of them:
// two or more tickets, each open, or coordd answers Thread. coordd answers it Place on the first and posts the same
// words as the owner's on the second, each post naming the other; further tickets get no post and are named to him;
// the owner picks nothing (the owner on N-354/N-355, paper-research N-586.p10). When two or more of the tickets
// given wait on the owner's own answer, nothing is posted on them: the thread kind makes the words a thread of their
// own, given at once to TO, with the tickets beside it (paper-research N-581.p57).

call intake(text: Text, to: Agent) -> Thread | Place(ticket: Ticket) | Unsure(tickets: [Ticket])
  task intake

call sort(text: Text, to: Agent, open: Text) -> Thread | Place(ticket: Ticket) | Unsure(tickets: [Ticket])
  task intake
  template
    The owner of a software team wrote the message below to {{ to }}, one of the team's agents. Say where it goes:

    - Place(ticket): it continues the subject of one of the open tickets listed after it: it answers or follows up
      what was said there, or is about the same piece of work. Sharing a file, a module or the agent is not enough; a
      different question about the same code is a Thread. A short answer ("yes", "go ahead", "looks good",
      "no, keep it") belongs on the ticket whose last post waits on the owner's answer, when exactly one does. Give the
      ticket's id as listed. A reply to a ticket that asks the owner whom a post is for belongs on that ticket:
      words that say whom ("for lead", "fixer should"), or the same message said again more clearly.
    - Unsure(tickets): it continues the subject of more than one of the open tickets, and the words do not say
      which. Give those tickets, the likeliest first. The message is then posted as the owner's on the first two
      tickets given, in the order given, each post naming the other; any further ones are only named to him. When
      two or more of the tickets given wait on the owner's answer, it is posted on none of them and becomes a thread
      of its own, with those tickets shown beside it.
    - Thread: anything else: a question, a remark, or a piece of work that none of the open tickets is about.

    The message:
    <<<
    {{ text }}
    >>>

    The open tickets:
    {{ open }}
